From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Date: Fri, 23 Sep 1994 05:42:37 GMT
Subject: [#94-9-403] What Happens First?

Robin Hanson writes:
> Nick clearly sees long meat lifespans and reproducing comet eaters 
> and AI as much easier than I do, relative [to] uploading for
> example.  Care to explain why those look easy?

I can't comment in Nick's reasons (which I haven't seen), but I tend to
agree with his conclusions.  I see uploading as very difficult because
of the problems associated with reading the synapse-neuron connectivity
and weighting data out of the wetware.

> How do comet eaters come so far before reproducing assemblers?

Will reproducing assemblers come at all?  Wee reproductive beasties may,
but I've never seen a convincing argument for how they will assemble
anything.  How are their actions coodinated?  I favour the idea of 
macroscale nanotech factories as more economically viable.

> How can we go past our apparent 120 year lifespan limit before
> nanotech?

Perhaps the human genome project pays off in age-slowing drugs/gene-
altering viruses before full-blown nanotech arrives.  Also it is not
clear if their really is a natural 120-year limit.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

