Subject: [#94-9-407] Quantum Erasers

On Thu, 22 Sept 1994, I said:

>> Speaking of which, I just finished reading _Cosmic Wormholes_ by
>> Paul Halpern.  Apparently, according to Kip Thorne et al., objects
>> near a wormhole lack well-defined positions and velocities until an
>> observation is made.


Mike Price responded:

>My first reaction is: isn't this true for objects anywhere?  Give me a page
>number from CW and I'll try to comment further.

The thing that struck me was that Thorne et al. were talking about 
*macroscopic* objects being fuzzed out over *macroscopic* distances.  (See 
pp. 189-90 of CW).  The book doesn't specify exact distances, but my 
impression is that a spaceship approaching a wormhole could have a 
positional uncertainty of (at least) several km!

>Second point is that a lot of work has been done on Hawking's chronology
>protection conjecture since the book was written, which makes it look less
>likely that wormholes can be used for time travel.

Where can I find out more about these recent developments?


